9-21/ecf335cf/", "sessions": null, "completed_at": "2026-09-21 01:55:00"}
 {"id": "BT-041", "title": "CLI contract: `<cmd> --help` exits 1 (the documented validation-failure code) on every subcommand, and `event` with no --type silently appends an audit event instead of refusing", "status": "pending", "priority": "P2", "reasoning": "MEASURED 2026-09-21 (PM cycle; published v0.1.7 asset, fresh temp board, 13 subcommands probed):\n(a) `boardctl <cmd> --help` exits 1 for every subcommand (create/update/event/header/show/list/import/render/serve/init/validate/doctor/stats) — the flag package's ErrHelp path flows out as a plain error — while bare `help` and `--help` exit 0. Any wrapper that classifies exit 1 as \"validation failure\" (README:192 \"Exit codes: 0 ok, 1 validation failure, 2 usage/board-not-found\") reads a help request as a failed board validation.\n(b) `boardctl event` with NO --type is not refused: it appends {\"event_type\":\"audit\",\"timestamp\":...,\"actor\":\"foreman\"} and exits 0, while the subcommand's own usage line (cmd/boardctl/main.go:653) advertises `event --type audit [flags]` and `event --type bogus` IS refused (exit 1, vocabulary listed). A typo'd or omitted type silently writes an audit event — the append-only audit trail takes a fact nobody asked for, with no error.\nREPRO COMMANDS: for c in create update event header show list import render serve init validate doctor stats; do /tmp/asset/boardctl_linux_amd64 $c --help; echo $?; done  -> rc=1 for all; bare `help` -> rc=0.\n  $B -C . event ; echo $? -> rc=0 ; tail -1 events.jsonl -> \"event_type\": \"audit\".\nPRIOR RECORD, NEVER FILED: DF-BOARDCTL-7's reasoning (complete row) says \"ALSO MEASURED (not filed separately): 'boardctl <cmd> --help' exits 1 for every subcommand while bare 'help'/'--help' exits 0\" — half of this row is a known, deliberately-deferred observation that never got its own row; the `event --type` half is new.\nWHY A ROW AND NOT A ONE-LINE FIX: the fix is a decision (which exit code a help request is), then a test that pins it across the whole subcommand set — every subcommand's help currently collides with the documented failure code, so one command's local fix would leave the contract asymmetric.\nAC: (1) the decided exit code for `<cmd> --help` is pinned by a test table covering EVERY subcommand, and README:192's taxonomy sentence names it; (2) `event` with no --type either documents an explicit default in its usage line or is refused — usage text and behavior must agree, with a test for whichever is chosen; (3) the published v0.1.7 asset (or the next cut) is re-probed and shows the decided codes, quote the probe.", "created_at": "2026-09-21T13:07:08Z", "updated_at": "2026-09-21T13:07:08Z", "worker_status": "pending", "detail": {"fingerprint": "5ffed9163df3abd68729f64938fd9933186220b1074a5483d582d28246525362"}, "ts": null, "complexity": 2, "foreman_note": null, "worktree": null, "branch": null, "commit_hash": null, "guard_result": null, "ci_result": null, "worker_summary": null, "sessions": null, "completed_at": null, "capability_tags": ["cli", "contract", "docs"]}
 {"id": "BT-042", "title": "DEPLOY RECURRENCE (BT-038 predicted): ~/.local/bin/boardctl is a dirty from-HEAD build (79a1012, lacks the DF-6 folder-upload fix, reports version dev) with no install target and no drift probe", "status": "pending", "priority": "P2", "reasoning": "MEASURED 2026-09-21 (PM cycle; read-only probes on the repo + the installed binary). This is the RECURRENCE BT-038's own row predicted for itself.\nFACTS:\n1. ~/.local/bin/boardctl mtime 2026-09-20 01:08. `go version -m ~/.local/bin/boardctl` -> mod ...v0.1.7-0.20260920060733-79a10123addd+dirty, vcs.revision=79a10123addd, vcs.modified=true. It is a gitignored, dirty, from-HEAD build — a DIFFERENT class of artifact from the README's primary install path (a SHA256SUMS-verified release asset that reports v0.1.7 and vcs.modified=false).\n2. BT-038's own completion named the refresh as \"rebuilt+installed ~/.local/bin/boardctl from merged main (incl. BT-037 fix + DF-1/3/4/5/7)\", but 79a1012 is the DF-BOARDCTL-2 merge and is NOT an ancestor of the DF-BOARDCTL-6 folder-upload fix: `git merge-base --is-ancestor 6189838 79a10123addd` -> rc=1. The installed copy therefore lacks the newest user-facing fix it claims to carry, and carries none of BT-039.\n3. `~/.local/bin/boardctl version --json` -> {\"version\":\"dev\",\"build\":\"dev\"}: a source build cannot even name its own revision, so drift is undetectable from the tool itself; `go version -m` is the only probe.\n4. Nothing documents or verifies an install: no install target in the Makefile (targets: build/test/vet/fmt/fmt-check/version-check/vuln-check/release/clean) and `grep -n 'local/bin' README.md Makefile docs/ skills/` has no hits. BT-038's own reasoning pre-declared this exact failure: \"a defined deploy step so ~/.local/bin/boardctl is refreshed when boardctl merges (today it is manual and therefore silently stale)\".\n5. Impact stays contained today: the boardctl-foreman-ops skill instructs foremen to build and use ./bin/boardctl before board writes, so foreman board writes do not use the stale copy. The exposure is any other caller of the PATH binary (sibling projects boards, operator one-offs) running a build with neither the DF-6 knobs nor the BT-039 hygiene.\n6. Deliberately NOT re-done in this cycle: a manual re-copy of the binary. Two writers building and installing into a live board repo while its tick lane can build is how the dirty-mtime confusion (BT-039) recurs; the durable half is the verification + provenance step, which is this row.\nAC: (1) an install target or a documented install step that installs the RELEASED asset by tag and verifies sha256, so the deployed binary is reproducible from the tag and identifiable; (2) a stated verification probe (e.g. `boardctl version --json` expected value, or `go version -m`) in README/Development so drift is detectable at any time, not just during a release audit; (3) after the next release, a probe shows the deployed binary reporting the released tag with vcs.modified=false and a revision that is an ancestor of that tag — quote the probe output; (4) the next cycle that re-cuts a release notes whether the installed copy moved, so this class gets a signal instead of a rediscovery.", "created_at": "2026-09-21T13:07:08Z", "updated_at": "2026-09-21T13:07:08Z", "worker_status": "pending", "detail": {"fingerprint": "637955a0964ae5ed87a200d77bd0672ad6f13b57a2ff1a7a256398c5b864894a"}, "ts": null, "complexity": 2, "foreman_note": null, "worktree": null, "branch": null, "commit_hash": null, "guard_result": null, "ci_result": null, "worker_summary": null, "sessions": null, "completed_at": null, "capability_tags": ["deploy", "install", "stale"]}
